<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kachin State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kachin State is the northernmost state of Myanmar, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with its capital at Myitkyina. Its mountains and the upper Irrawaddy valley are home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-people">Kachin peoples</a> and to Shan and Burman communities, and it includes the jade mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hpakant">Hpakant</a>. It was formed in 1948 and has been a centre of armed conflict between the Kachin Independence Army and the Burmese military since the 1960s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kachin-state.jpg" alt="Kachin State" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saimawnkham · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moengkaung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kachin State covers 89,041 square kilometres. It is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the north and east, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> to the south, and by Sagaing Region and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arunachal-pradesh">Arunachal Pradesh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to the west. Besides Myitkyina, its capital, its important towns include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhamo">Bhamo</a>, Mohnyin and Putao. The state holds Myanmar's highest mountain, Hkakabo Razi, at 5,889 metres, and a large inland lake, Indawgyi Lake. Its economy is predominantly agricultural, and its minerals include gold and jade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hpakant">Hpakant</a> is well known for its jade mines, and Bhamo is one of the border trading points with China.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Nanzhao Kingdom controlled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>, including the area of the present state, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a> afterwards administered the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-people">Kachin people</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> briefly controlled it during the Sino-Burmese War of 1760. The region was historically inhabited by many groups, among them the Rawang, Lisu, Jingpo and Zaiwa, and the term Kachin was given by the British in the colonial period. Traditional society rested on shifting hill agriculture, with political authority held by chieftains, and the people were animist. The British accepted the autonomy of the Kachin Hills, though their forces carried out expeditions against the Kachin in 1892 and 1896.</p><p>On 12 February 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panglong-conference">Panglong Agreement</a> with the Shan, Kachin and Chin peoples, which envisioned a Kachin State. Burma became independent on 4 January 1948, and the state was formed the same year from the former civil districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhamo">Bhamo</a> and Myitkyina and the northern district of Puta-o. The northern frontier was not demarcated until the 1960s. After the military coup of 1962 Kachin forces, who had formed a significant part of the Burmese army, withdrew and formed the Kachin Independence Army. The state was virtually independent from the mid-1960s through 1994, when an army offensive seized the jade mines and a ceasefire was signed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kachin State had a population of 1,689,441 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2014. Its mountain hinterlands are mostly Kachin, while the railway corridor and southern valleys are mostly Shan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Bamar</a>. Buddhists form the largest religious community, with a large Christian minority. Burmese and Jingpho are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua francas</a>, and a railway links Myitkyina with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kachin State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mogaung, Shan Mong Kawng, on the Mogaung river in present-day <b>Kachin State</b>, was one of the northern Shan states; Shan chronicles date the founding of its ruling house to the early 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/mogaung">Mogaung</a></li><li><blockquote>Mohnyin, Shan Mong Yang, lay in the upper Irrawaddy basin in what is now <b>Kachin State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohnyin">Mohnyin</a></li></ul></section>
